# View {{ name }} traces in Langfuse

The generated HTTP service already uses the library's optional tracing adapter.
Your graph stays ordinary LangGraph. Each accepted response can link to its model
calls, tool calls and checkpoint references in a separate Langfuse service.

## 1. Get your project settings

Ask the platform team for a Langfuse HTTPS URL, project ID, public key and secret
key. For a local backend, use its loopback URL, such as `http://localhost:3000`.
The project ID is the value after `/project/` in its browser URL.

Create a private file, for example `F:\work\langfuse\agent.env`:

```dotenv
{{ var.langfuse_enabled }}=true
{{ var.langfuse_base_url }}=http://localhost:3000
{{ var.langfuse_project_id }}=your-project-id
{{ var.langfuse_public_key }}=your-project-public-key
{{ var.langfuse_secret_key }}=your-project-secret-key
{{ var.langfuse_capture_content }}=false
```

Set `{{ var.langfuse_capture_content }}=true` when using approved test data to
inspect model messages and tool arguments/results. With `false`, traces contain
metadata, token counts and checkpoint IDs. Keep real keys and captured content
out of source control. The browser receives a private trace link, never API keys.

## 2. Start the agent

Open Command Prompt at the workspace root and select Python as described in the
[workspace README](../../../README.md#select-python). The generated dependencies
include the core `otel` extra; the installer uses Python and pip.

```bat
"%PYTHON%" -m ai_agent_lib_cli install
set "{{ var.langfuse_env_file }}=F:\work\langfuse\agent.env"
"%PYTHON%" -m ai_agent_lib_cli config explain {{ name }}
"%PYTHON%" -m ai_agent_lib_cli doctor {{ name }}
"%PYTHON%" -m {{ package }}.service --port {{ port }}
```

Use your file's actual path. Process variables override that file, which overrides
`agents/{{ name }}/.env`. Restart the agent after changing settings.
`config explain` identifies each setting's source and masks both API keys.
`doctor` validates the same settings as startup; this configuration check does
not contact Langfuse or export content.

The backend runs separately. A developer connecting to a shared backend needs
Python, pip and network access to it. No Langfuse server runs inside the agent or
Agent Lab process. Operational telemetry keeps its existing destination.

## 3. Inspect a conversation

Select **{{ name }}** in Agent Lab. Ask `My name is Ada. Please greet me.` and
then `What is my name?` in the same chat. Use a real model to exercise tool choices;
the default fake model echoes input, while the tests script its tool calls.

Open **Open trace in Langfuse** beneath either answer and sign in to Langfuse.
Each question has its own trace and request ID. The two traces share a session
scoped to the caller, tenant, agent and thread. After a tool runs, a later model
input shows its result. A follow-up's model input shows the saved conversation.
Checkpoint IDs appear in root metadata; the checkpoint database stays with the
agent. Token/cache counts remain visible in Agent Lab and survive a chat reload.

Tracing covers `POST /invoke`, including Agent Lab's live response mode. The
standalone CLI and `/invoke/stream` graph-step endpoint keep their existing
behavior. Export happens in the background; a link may appear before ingestion
finishes. An exporter outage does not retry a business request or discard its
answer. To disable export, set `{{ var.langfuse_enabled }}=false` and restart.
Checkpoint reference collection has a one-second deadline and a 64-reference
limit. Missing or truncated references are marked in trace metadata; a slow
diagnostic lookup does not indefinitely delay the accepted answer.
